WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:20.560
[Background music]

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:27.480
Narrator: Meet Ike Isodope, photofluorograph operator first-class. A pretty smart guy. 

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:32.060
He's got a mighty important job helping the doctors in the health department

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:35.200
to find cases of tuberculosis and other chest diseases.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:40.540
Every day hundreds of people line up at Ike's PF machine to get a chest x-ray. 

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:46.340
Every day he shoots the x-rays as fast as the equipment can take it. 

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:52.700
Yes, Ike's a first class operator and a valuable guy to have around in x-ray work. 

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:59.000
He knows his equipment cold. He knows what it can do and what it can't do. 

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:05.120
He's never burned out a tube before its time or shorted out a power line, ever since he's been doing x-ray work. 

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:08.800
Ike certainly takes care of his PF machine. 

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:14.320
The big question, though, is how well has Ike been taking care of himself? 

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:26.380
He's got a protective lead screen, which can stop x-ray radiation cold, but does he use it? The answer is no. 

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:36.280
He figures he can save a couple of steps a day by reaching for the control button. In order to make life exciting, Ike plays games. 

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:40.460
His favorite one is peek-a-boo with the stray x-rays surrounding his PF unit. 

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:45.520
He'll lose though, in the end. 

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:52.360
Sometimes Ike likes to play the hero. He lets his positioner shoot him for test purposes, 

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:57.560
but they don't hand out gold medals for this kind of heroism. 

00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:01.920
During his rest periods Ike doesn't like to go far from his machine. 

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:07.740
He's conscientious all right, but not very bright about the places he picks to take his smoke,

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:15.280
right alongside a busy machine or in the unprotected cab of a trailer unit. 

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:18.880
You see, no one ever put a strong enough label on Ike's equipment,

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:23.000
so he's not fully aware of the danger involved in x-ray work. 

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:28.370
Now with high-tension cables, transformers, and switches it's different.

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:34.360
That you've got to respect, says Ike, because they can kill you faster than you can blink an eye. 

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:40.680
But x-rays, says Ike, I haven't seen anyone keel over from them yet.

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:44.770
But it could happen, and here's why.

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:49.030
[Background music]

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:55.900
Like arsenic, the poisonous effects of excessive exposure to X-ray radiation are cumulative.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:59.920
Take a little today and a little tomorrow and you won't feel it at all,

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:04.160
but go on taking it for a long time and it'll make you very sick.

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:07.450
Overexposure to x-rays can make you anemic. 

00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:11.090
It can give you a troublesome and painful skin condition. 

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:16.650
It can result in cancer of the skin or if you soak up enough of the x-rays, 

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:21.840
it can result in sterility or genetic damage. 

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:26.740
It won't happen overnight, but carelessness or negligence in protecting yourself

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:31.900
against the harmful effects of x-rays will catch up with you eventually. 

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:35.780
It can kill you long before your time. 

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:42.920
Medical authorities agree that you can take a small amount of x-ray radiation each week without harmful effects,

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:48.950
but you should not have more than 3/10 of a roentgen per week. 

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:53.920
The important thing, if you want to stay well on the job for your entire career, 

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:57.380
is that you know the facts about radiation hazards. 

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:01.580
Learn the rules of safety and follow them. 

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:04.480
Here are the facts.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:12.320
First, remember that a PF unit normally takes 10 times as many x-ray films in a day's work as a 14 by 17 inch unit. 

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:16.980
So that the danger of exposure is multiplied many times over. 

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:23.920
Second, remember that every small film photofluorograph requires about 20 times

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:30.020
the amount of x-rays used in conventional 14 by 17 inch radiography.

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:36.980
Thus, the danger of overexposure to x-rays is far greater in a PF unit than in a radiographic installation. 

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:42.440
Both can be safe, but one takes more care than the others. 

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:49.160
Above all, don't be fooled by the fact that your PF machine uses a ray-proof x-ray tube. 

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:53.140
It is true that proper coning and proper alignment of the tube

00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:57.820
will keep the primary x-ray beam from spilling off the PF screen. 

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:01.110
So if you're operating the equipment properly,

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:04.640
you don't have to worry much about exposure to primary radiation,

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:08.940
but you do have to protect yourself against secondary radiation. 

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:13.820
With the primary beam aimed directly at the photofluographic stream; 

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:16.920
there will be only a slight amount of secondary radiation. 

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:22.500
However, with the patient in position, 

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:26.080
there will be a larger amount of secondary radiation. 

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:31.680
The patient himself increases the amount of secondary radiation by about five times. 

00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:37.140
Through the bird's eye view of the PF installation showing the control box and lead screen,

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:40.900
the x-ray tube, and the photofluorographic hood,

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:43.180
with the patient in position,

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:50.360
let's see how much secondary radiation there is at various points and how great a hazard it actually is. 

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:58.360
A careless person standing beside the tube would exceed safe radiation after only 75 films per week. 

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:05.620
Standing beside the patient he would exceed the limits of safe radiation after only 100 films.

00:06:05.780 --> 00:06:09.680
A careless person standing beside the lead screen could take

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:15.400
only 150 films a week before he exceeded 3/10 of a roentgen. 

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:21.880
Even at a position 15 feet from the x-ray tube, he'd be in danger after 3,000 films. 

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:27.800
However, behind the lead screen or at a distance of at least 20 feet,

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:34.280
the person would not exceed 3/10 of a roentgen per week until after 7,500 exposures were made. 

00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:40.600
Yes, the photofluographic unit can be a safe place to work.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:44.500
To be safe, you need follow only a few simple safety rules. 

00:06:45.340 --> 00:06:49.580
Never remove the cone or diaphragm from the tube housing. 

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:55.440
Always be sure that your primary x-ray beam is properly coned

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:58.200
and the tube is properly aligned. 

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:02.180
An improperly coned or poorly aligned primary x-ray beam

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:06.220
can increase secondary radiation by two to three times. 

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:13.360
In setting up your PF equipment, make sure that the x-rays are beamed toward a window or an outside wall. 

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:17.720
Don't make x-ray targets of your next door neighbor.

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:21.280
Walls don't serve as fool-proof shields.

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:26.420
Be sure that all persons working in the PF units are behind the lead screens

00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:31.420
or at a safe distance from the equipment during every x-ray exposure. 

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:35.360
Be sure the control stand is behind the lead screen so that you're

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:39.960
completely protected when you make the x-ray exposure. 

00:07:39.960 --> 00:07:45.260
Make sure that all protective screens are tall enough to shield you from head to toe. 

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:51.700
Don't play peek-a-boo with secondary radiation. Protect yourself fully. 

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:53.940
Expose not even a hand or a finger. 

00:07:54.740 --> 00:08:00.760
Wait until your positioner has reached the safety of the protective screen before making the x-ray exposure. 

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:08.340
A foot switch for the positioner in series with the operator's control switch can be a valuable device. 

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:15.400
Placed behind the lead screen, it will prevent exposure until the positioner has reached a safe position. 

00:08:16.600 --> 00:08:19.320
Don't neglect your hostess or secretary.

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:23.660
Station her at a safe distance, at least 20 feet from the PF machine

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:27.960
where she can greet your customers without danger to herself or to them. 

00:08:29.260 --> 00:08:34.660
On your release time, don't hang around the PF unit while it's in operation.

00:08:34.900 --> 00:08:37.940
Stay behind the lead screen,

00:08:38.940 --> 00:08:45.460
or better yet take your smoke or your coffee out of range of the x-rays. 

00:08:46.280 --> 00:08:52.640
Don't be a hero to test your equipment, and don't ask your positioner to stand in for your test shot. 

00:08:52.880 --> 00:09:00.520
One of these test shots is enough to put you way over your safety limit of 3/10 of a roentgen per week. 

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:05.160
Above all, keep tabs on the amount of radiation you're absorbing

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:08.400
with a strip of dental film or some other detection device.

00:09:08.700 --> 00:09:09.520
Wear it. 

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:13.780
Each week have it processed and read by an expert. 

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:17.660
The density of the film will show whether the radiation

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:20.750
to which you've been exposed is safe or dangerous.

00:09:20.920 --> 00:09:23.250
If you've had too much exposure, find out why. 

00:09:24.550 --> 00:09:27.950
Furthermore, while you're working with x-ray equipment,

00:09:27.950 --> 00:09:30.830
your blood count should be checked at least once every six months. 

00:09:32.140 --> 00:09:37.240
Have a periodic check of actual radiation conditions with an ionization chamber. 

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:41.860
This will help to point up exposure hazards before injury can occur. 

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:48.240
Remember, you can avoid injury and stay healthy by avoiding exposure.

00:09:48.820 --> 00:09:54.120
Learn the rules of photofluorographic safety and live up to them. 

00:09:54.380 --> 00:09:58.240
A photofluorographic unit can be a safe place in which to work, 

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:05.240
but it is safe only when you who work in it take every precaution against x-ray exposure.

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:07.230
[Background music]

00:10:07.380 --> 00:10:15.000
[The End]

